      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Note",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# TEST: EPS Checkpoint Switcher - same prompt, every model, one Run\n\n**Press Run right now and it finishes clean without doing anything.** That\nis not a bug, it is the first thing worth knowing: with nothing ticked, the\nqueue succeeds and everything downstream is simply skipped. No red node, no\nerror, no output.\n\nNow tick a checkpoint - or three - and Run again. You get one full\ngeneration per ticked model, same prompt, same seed, same everything, so the\nonly thing that differs between the pictures is the model itself.\n\n## Why this instead of a Checkpoint Loader\n\nA normal loader gives you one model. To compare three you would wire three\nloaders, three pairs of text encoders and three samplers, then keep them all\nin step by hand.\n\nThis is one node. Tick three files, the rest of the graph runs three times.\n\nAnd `model`, `clip` and `vae` come out **matched by construction**: the CLIP\nand VAE handed downstream on run two are the ones that came out of run two's\nfile. You cannot accidentally pair one model's weights with another model's\nVAE, because they never travel apart.\n\n## The label output\n\nThe fourth output is `label` - the ticked file's name with the folder and\nextension stripped - and it is wired straight into Save Image's\n`filename_prefix`. So results land as\n\n    output/<model name>_00001_.png\n\none name per model, and you never have to guess which picture came from\nwhich file. Unwire it and type your own prefix if you would rather.\n\n## Setup\n\nNone. Tick things. The list is your own `models/checkpoints` folder, grouped\nby subfolder, with a filter box at the top for when it gets long. A file\nthat has been deleted since you ticked it shows a warning marker and is\nskipped rather than killing the run.\n\n## Start small\n\nEvery ticked checkpoint really is loaded and really does generate. Three at\n20 steps is a coffee. Ten SDXL checkpoints is an afternoon and a lot of RAM.\nTick three the first time, watch the clock, then get ambitious.\n\n## Worth changing\n\n- **The prompt.** It is shared across every model - that is the point.\n- **steps down to 8** for a fast rough comparison, then back up once you\n  know which two models are worth a proper look.\n- **The seed off `fixed`** - but then both the model *and* the noise change\n  between pictures and you have learned nothing. Keep it fixed while you are\n  comparing.\n- **512 x 512** in Empty Latent is deliberately small so the first run is\n  quick. Put it up once you trust the setup.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        0,
